-only task (\"repositories and compute are not required\").\n- No CAMUV materials (source, papers, README, configs, data) exist in the workspace; filesystem search returned nothing.\n- Environment: Python 3.14.4, **no** numpy/scipy/sklearn/pandas, no causal packages, network disabled, 224 CPUs, no GPU, datasets=[].\n\n**Working formulation of CAMUV (ASSUMPTION \u2014 from literature recall, must be re-verified):**\n- CAMUV = *CAusal Models with Unobserved Variables* (Kunzmann & Maathuis, UAI 2021, arXiv:2011.00664).\n- Model class (causal additive model with unobserved variables):\n  X_j = \u03a3_{k \u2208 PA_j} f_{jk}(X_k) + U_j, for j = 1..p,\n  where the X_j are **observed**, each U_j is an **unobserved** continuous random variable, the U_j are (assumed) mutually independent with strictly positive joint density, and each U_j is additionally allowed to be a parent of *other* observed variables. The latter mechanism is precisely how **unobserved common causes** U \u2192 X_a, U \u2192 X_b (with no direct X_a\u2192X_b edge) arise in the data.\n- **Support S:** for each observed j, the set of unobserved variables U_l that are parents of X_j (S_j \u2282 {1..p}, with j \u2208 S_j always since U_j drives X_j). S encodes the latent structure.\n- **Identifiability claim (ASSUMPTION):** given the support S, the causal DAG among the observed variables is identifiable from the observed joint distribution, under conditions on the nonlinear additive functions f_{jk} (non-degenerate, e.g., linearly independent second derivatives \u2014 a \"nonlinearity\" condition).\n- **Estimation (ASSUMPTION):** two-step procedure \u2014 (i) *support inference* via variable selection in penalized additive models; (ii) *DAG search* restricted to DAGs compatible with the estimated support, scored by a residual-based local score (independence of residuals after additive-model regression), so residual dependence is attributed to a common cause rather than a direct edge.\n\n**Explicit separation:** Section 0 facts are verified. Everything in this paragraph is a literature-based assumption that must be re-confirmed against the primary source before implementation, because no copy is available locally and network is disabled. Any deviation found (e.g., different score, search over orderings vs DAGs, different support-inference routine) is to be absorbed into the design as a parameter, not silently changed.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Operational definition of \"flagging a latent common cause\" (explicit design choice)\n\nA latent common cause is an unobserved U_l that parents \u2265 2 observed variables. The experiment needs an observable, checkable operational target.\n\n**Design choice (grounded in the CAMUV residual-attribution logic):**\n- For any observed pair (a,b) with **no direct edge** in the true observed DAG, the pair is a *candidate latent pair*.\n- The algorithm \"flags a latent common cause\" for (a,b) when both of the following hold **and are checked** (they are outputs of the pipeline, not assumptions of the data):\n  1. The estimated observed DAG contains **no direct edge** between a and b, and\n  2. A **latent-flag** is produced by the support/residual analysis: after regressing X_b on its estimated observed-parent set, the residuals are still dependent on X_a (HSIC/dCorr above threshold), i.e., the residual dependence is attributed to an unobserved driver rather than to a direct edge.\n\n**Justification:** this matches CAMUV's design philosophy (residual dependence \u21d2 unobserved confounder, not an edge) and is implementable with the same residual machinery used for scoring. The alternative (outputting the identity/effect of U_l) is not identifiable and is deliberately out of scope.\n\n**Evaluation metric for the target:** *latent-pair detection* precision and recall over all candidate latent pairs:\n- detection precision = (# flagged (a,b) that are truly latent pairs) / (# flagged pairs)\n- detection recall = (# flagged (a,b) that are truly latent pairs) / (# true latent pairs)\n- plus a **direct-vs-latent disambiguation** rate: among pairs that are marginally strongly correlated but not directly connected, the fraction correctly classified as \"latent-caused\" rather than given a spurious direct edge.\n\nThe threshold in (2) is a design hyperparameter (e.g., HSIC p-value < 0.05 under permutation test, or dCorr > 0.1), fixed before experiments and swept once for sensitivity.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Data-generating process (synthetic, inside the CAMUV model class)\n\n**Ground truth is fully known because we generate it.** Each configuration is defined by a config object:\n- **Observed DAG:** Erd\u0151s\u2013R\u00e9nyi or random order-based DAG over p observed variables, average degree ~1\u20132, acyclic by construction (random permutation order with edges only from lower to higher index).\n- **Link functions (identified regime):** per-edge f chosen randomly from {sin(2x), x\u00b3, 5\u00b7sigmoid(x), cubic B-spline, polynomial of degree 2\u20134} with random sign/scale so that links are genuinely nonlinear. A **linear-only arm** (f(x)=a\u00b7x) is generated as the expected non-identifiable/failure boundary.\n- **Latent variables:** U_j ~ N(0,1), independent across j (positive-density, independent \u2014 satisfies the assumed identifiability condition).\n- **Latent confounding injection:** each U_j, with probability p_share (a control), additionally parents a small random subset of other observed variables (degree 1\u20132), with nonlinear link. This creates true latent common causes. A dedicated worked example (Section 7) sets exactly one common cause.\n- **Observational data:** n i.i.d. samples drawn from the model; noise-free (no extra additive error beyond U_j, which is the model's noise).\n- **Sample sizes / graph sizes:**\n  - Exact/exhaustive arm: p \u2208 {4, 5} with n \u2208 {200, 500, 1000, 2000}.\n  - Semi-exact arm: p = 6 with support-pruned enumeration.\n  - Greedy/randomized arm: p \u2208 {10, 20, 50}, sparse, n \u2208 {500, 1000, 2000}.\n- **Perturbation axes (full factorial where cheap, otherwise latin-hypercube):** confounding strength (p_share and the scale of latent\u2192observed links: weak/moderate/strong), number of induced latent-common-cause edges, linear vs nonlinear links, and sample size n.\n- **Replication:** \u2265 30 seeds per configuration for the exact arm (fewer, \u2265 10, for large-p), random graphs/functions regenerated per seed.\n\n**Rationale:** synthetic in-model data is the only setup where the ground-truth DAG, support S, and common-cause set are known \u2014 required to evaluate identifiability and recovery. Generating *inside* the model tests whether CAMUV recovers the truth; generating on the linear arm and on extreme confounding tests the boundary of the assumed identifiability conditions.\n\n---\n\n## 4. CAMUV implementation (self-contained, honest feasibility)\n\n**Hard constraint (verified):** no numpy/scipy and no network, so nothing can be installed. The design therefore mandates a **self-contained implementation**: either (a) vendor a minimal subset of numpy/scipy operations into the workspace as pure-Python modules (B-spline basis, triangular solves, SVD for low rank), or (b) pure-stdlib `math`/`statistics` implementations restricted to the small-p core. All numeric kernels (spline basis, group-lasso/boosting fit, residual computation, HSIC/dCorr, graph enumeration) are explicit modules with unit tests on tiny known graphs.\n\n**Pipeline modules:**\n1. **Additive-model variable selection (support inference).** For each target j, fit X_j on a B-spline basis per candidate predictor with a group-lasso/componentwise-boosting sparsity penalty; the selected predictor set is the estimated support pattern. (This also gives the parent-candidate sets used below.)\n2. **Residual-based scoring kernel.** Residual independence measure: HSIC (empirical, with Gaussian kernel, permutation-based p-value) or distance correlation dCorr, implemented from scratch. This kernel is reused for both DAG scoring and latent-flagging (Section 2) so the two targets share one code path.\n3. **DAG search.**\n   - p \u2264 5: **exhaustive enumeration** of all DAGs (543 for p=4, 29,281 for p=5), restricted to those compatible with the estimated support; score each and pick the minimizer. Verifiable and complete \u2014 this is the gold-standard arm.\n   - p = 6: enumerate support-compatible DAGs (a much smaller, pruned set than all 3.7M DAGs); if still too large, fall back to the greedy arm.\n   - p \u2265 10: greedy/randomized search \u2014 random-restart hill climbing over parent sets compatible with the support, using the same score. **Caveat stated explicitly:** results in this arm depend on heuristic search quality, so large-p recovery is reported separately from exact-recovery arms and never treated as proof of identifiability.\n4. **Latent-flagging (Section 2).** For support-compatible pairs with no direct edge, test residual dependence of X_a on X_b after regression on observed parents; flag if significant.\n\n**Feasibility checks (design-time, cheap, not experiment results):** (i) the spline/group-lasso module reproduces univariate regression on a hand-built 1-D example; (ii) the HSIC kernel returns ~0 for independent Gaussians and >0 for correlated ones; (iii) exhaustive enumeration of p=5 DAGs completes in the exact arm within the CPU/time budget (estimate below). These checks gate the rest of the run.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Controls, ablations, and analysis plan\n\n**Ablations (fixed factor in every arm):**\n- **A1 Full CAMUV** vs **A2 CAM** (B\u00fchlmann et al., additive noise without latent handling \u2014 implemented from the same modules by disabling latent-flagging/support treatment and assuming no latents). This directly demonstrates the added value of latent-awareness under common causes.\n- **A3 known support** vs **A4 estimated support**: run the DAG search with the true S plugged in vs S inferred in step (4.1), isolating support-inference error from DAG-search error.\n- **A5 linear-only links** (expected failure arm): verifies that the nonlinearity identifiability condition actually matters.\n- **A6 no-latent sanity control:** generate *without* any shared latent parents; CAMUV should behave like CAM and not flag spurious latents (measures false-positive rate of the latent flag).\n\n**Control variables:** same seeds across ablations; same graphs/functions within a seed across A1\u2013A6; scoring threshold fixed in advance (single sensitivity sweep reported separately). A permutation-based ground-truth \"random DAG\" null (score distribution under shuffled data) is reported to show the score separates truth from noise.\n\n**Metrics (aggregated as mean \u00b1 std over seeds):**\n- Structure: **SHD** (structural Hamming distance), **SID** (structural intervention distance) vs true DAG.\n- Edge recovery: precision/recall on edges (skeleton) and **orientation accuracy** (fraction of correctly oriented recovered edges).\n- Latent handling: **latent-pair detection** precision/recall and **direct-vs-latent disambiguation rate** (Section 2).\n- Support estimation: accuracy/F1 of the estimated S vs true S (for A3/A4 comparison).\n- Primary output: *success-rate* (SHD=0 with correct latent flags) vs n curves for the exact arm; tables for the large-p arm.\n\n**Analysis plan:** (i) success-rate vs n per configuration (does recovery approach 1 as n grows, in line with identifiability?); (ii) SHD/SID distributions per ablation \u2014 the \u0394(CAMUV \u2212 CAM) and \u0394(known\u2212estimated support) quantify the benefit of latent handling and the cost of support inference; (iii) failure-boundary plots: recovery vs confounding strength and vs number of latents, and the linear-link collapse (A5); (iv) one worked trace (Section 7) as the narrative answer.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Feasibility, compute budget, risks\n\n- **Compute:** 224 CPUs, no GPU. Exact arm p=5: 29k DAGs \u00d7 score each (residuals at n \u2264 2000, pure-Python HSIC). Estimated worst-case ~10\u2076\u201310\u2077 kernel evaluations per seed; budgeted ~10\u201340 CPU-minutes/seed if kernels are vectorized; 30 seeds \u2248 a few CPU-hours \u2014 comfortably within budget. p=6 pruned and large-p greedy arms are much cheaper per seed. The full matrix is parallelized over the 224 cores.\n- **Risks and mitigations:**\n  - *No packages/network (verified):* vendored or stdlib numerics; scope of large-p arm reduced if kernels are slow.\n  - *Identifiability/algorithm details from memory:* re-verify formulation before coding the score; the design parameterizes the score/search so deviations are localized.\n  - *Heuristic search at large p:* keep large-p results labeled as heuristic-quality dependent; never conflate with the exact arms.\n  - *Exhaustive search runtime at p=6:* pruning by support compatibility, with a documented fallback to the greedy arm.\n- **Deliverable artifacts (planned):** config-matrix JSON, modular code (generator, support inference, scoring kernel, search, evaluator), aggregation scripts, CSV results, and plots (success-vs-n, SHD/SID boxplots, failure-boundary curves, worked-example residual heatmap + recovered DAG figure).\n\n---\n\n## 7. Worked illustrative example (the narrative answer)\n\nExact p=4 graph: true unobserved common cause U\u2082 \u2192 X\u2082, U\u2082 \u2192 X\u2083 (so U\u2082 parents two observed variables; U\u2081\u2192X\u2081, U\u2083\u2192X\u2083, U\u2084\u2192X\u2084 are the per-variable latents) and one real observed edge X\u2081 \u2192 X\u2083, a chain X\u2081\u2192X\u2083\u2192X\u2084. Ground-truth observed-DAG edges: {X\u2081\u2192X\u2083, X\u2083\u2192X\u2084}; no X\u2082\u2013X\u2083 edge even though X\u2082 and X\u2083 are strongly marginally correlated (via U\u2082).\n\nExpected demonstration (the *predictions* the experiment is designed to show, not claimed results):\n- Marginal-correlation/CAM-style analysis would find X\u2082\u2013X\u2083 dependence and likely add a spurious direct edge between X\u2082 and X\u2083.\n- CAMUV, via the residual-attribution logic, should output **no direct edge** between X\u2082 and X\u2083 and **flag the latent common cause** U\u2082 (Section 2 criterion), while correctly recovering X\u2081\u2192X\u2083\u2192X\u2084.\n- The figure/counterexample (residual-dependence heatmap before/after latent-aware scoring, recovered vs true DAG) is the primary visual that answers the question of *how* CAMUV handles unobserved common causes.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Fixed requirements vs assumptions (summary)\n- **Fixed (task/environment):** design-only; no execution/claims; empty workspace; no packages; no network; Python 3.14; 224 CPU; no GPU; ground truth must come from our own generator.\n- **Assumptions (verify against paper):** CAMUV formulation; independent positive-density U_j; nonlinear-link identifiability condition; known/estimable support S; two-step estimation recipe; residual-independence scoring validity; the definitions/chosen thresholds in Section 2.", "evidence": "Workspace inspection (execute_check + inspect_repository): /workspace contains only task.json, whose documents.track.md says \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" `find / -iname '*camuv*'` returned nothing; no PDF/TeX found. Environment checks: Python 3.14.4; `import numpy/scipy/sklearn/pandas` all raised ModuleNotFoundError; no causal packages in pip list; environment metadata shows network disabled, gpu_enabled=false, cpu_affinity_count=224, datasets=[]. DAG-count enumeration (known combinatorics, used in feasibility): p=4 \u2192 543 DAGs, p=5 \u2192 29,281 DAGs, p=6 \u2192 ~3.7M DAGs, justifying exhaustive p\u22645 and pruned/greedy for larger p. Section 1 formulation is literature recall (Kunzmann & Maathuis UAI 2021) explicitly flagged as unverified assumption, not sourced from the workspace.", "assumptions": ["CAMUV is Kunzmann & Maathuis (UAI 2021, arXiv:2011.00664): causal additive model X_j = sum f_jk(X_k) + U_j with independent unobserved U_j of positive density, allowed to be parents of multiple observed variables (hence latent common causes).", "Identifiability of the observed DAG holds given known/estimable support S, nonlinear (non-degenerate) additive links f_jk, and independent positive-density U_j.", "Estimation is a two-step recipe: support inference via penalized additive-model variable selection, then residual-based DAG search restricted to support-compatible DAGs.", "Residual independence (HSIC/dCorr) is a valid scoring kernel for both DAG search and latent-common-cause flagging.", "Standard metrics (SHD, SID, precision/recall, orientation accuracy) are accepted; large-p results reflect heuristic search quality, not identifiability proofs.", "Implementation can be fully self-contained (vendored or pure-stdlib numerics) given absent packages and disabled network."], "uncertainties": ["Exact CAMUV notation and algorithmic details (score definition, ordering-vs-DAG search, support-inference routine) are recalled from literature and must be re-verified against the paper, which is absent from the workspace and unreachable (no network).", "No reference implementation or benchmark exists locally; official R code cannot be installed.", "Feasibility of exhaustive p=6 support-pruned enumeration in pure Python is unverified until implemented; fallback to greedy search is designed.", "The Section 2 latent-flagging threshold and metric are design choices; their calibration is a sensitivity sweep, not a resolved fact.", "Runtime estimates for pure-stdlib HSIC on large-n exact arms are approximate and will be confirmed by design-time feasibility checks."]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-e604bfc337a547aabd31a9c456af8f97", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Deliver full final CAMUV experiment design", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
